P["4708da64-0d90-4557-8eb1-3dcf1c8daa2d"] = [  # metastability
    "A flip-flop is a bistable loop with a balance point between 0 and 1; a setup/hold violation can leave it near that point.",
    "Metastability: the output hovers at an invalid level and resolves after an unbounded, random time (probability of still being unresolved decays as e^(−t/τ)).",
    "It cannot be eliminated because any continuous input-to-output mapping onto two stable states has a boundary region; you can only make failure less likely.",
    "Two-flip-flop synchroniser: the first flop may go metastable, but it gets a full clock period to resolve before the second samples it, so the chance of passing on a bad value falls exponentially.",
    "MTBF = e^(t_r/τ) / (T_0 f_clk f_data): it grows exponentially with resolution time t_r.",
    "Improve MTBF with more synchroniser stages, a slower synchroniser clock, fast flops (small τ), and minimal logic between stages; use handshakes or async FIFOs for buses.",
]
P["05aa0d71-354f-483b-afcd-a089d52595ac"] = [  # Moore vs Mealy
    "Moore: outputs depend on state only; Mealy: on state and current inputs.",
    "Mealy usually needs fewer states (the output can be given on the transition), e.g. a 101 detector needs 3 Mealy states versus 4 Moore.",
    "Timing: Mealy outputs respond in the same cycle as the input; Moore outputs change one clock later, aligned with the clock edge.",
    "Glitches: Mealy outputs follow input changes and glitches through combinational logic; Moore outputs are stable for the whole cycle.",
    "Prefer Mealy when latency or state count matters; prefer Moore for clean, synchronous outputs driving other clocked logic or external pins.",
    "Registering a Mealy output (a flip-flop on the output) removes glitches and gives Moore-like timing at the cost of one cycle of latency.",
]
P["a0a6260d-27af-447e-b30d-99a72869f79c"] = [  # FPGA vs MCU
    "An MCU executes instructions sequentially; throughput is limited by clock rate and instruction count per sample.",
    "An FPGA implements the algorithm as hardware: many operations run in parallel and pipelined each clock, giving far higher throughput and deterministic latency.",
    "LUT: a k-input LUT is a 2^k-bit memory addressed by the inputs; its configured contents are the truth table, so it implements any k-input function, with a flip-flop for registered outputs.",
    "Cost and power: MCUs are cheap and low power at rest; FPGAs cost more and draw more static power but can be more energy-efficient per operation for parallel DSP.",
    "Flexibility and effort: both are reprogrammable, but FPGA design (HDL, timing closure, verification) takes more skill and time than C on an MCU.",
    "Judgement: for high-rate parallel signal processing the FPGA (or an FPGA with a soft or hard CPU) is usually the right choice; for control tasks an MCU wins.",
]
P["8b136cf3-69af-4e4a-8d3d-0c5322b72bf0"] = [  # RISC vs CISC
    "RISC: fixed-length, simple instructions, few addressing modes, load/store memory access, many general-purpose registers; easy to decode and pipeline.",
    "CISC: variable-length instructions, many addressing modes, arithmetic can operate directly on memory; denser code and fewer instructions per program.",
    "Performance equation: time = instructions × CPI × clock period. RISC trades more instructions for lower CPI and faster clocks.",
    "Modern x86 decodes CISC instructions into RISC-like micro-operations executed by a pipelined, out-of-order core.",
    "So the distinction has moved: it now matters mainly at the ISA level (decode complexity, power, code density) rather than in the execution core.",
    "Conclusion: still meaningful for decoder cost and energy (relevant in mobile/embedded, favouring ARM/RISC-V), less so for peak performance.",
]
P["e87eef61-a0d3-40a8-8914-f645469dd7fe"] = [  # hardwired vs microprogrammed
    "Hardwired control: control signals generated by combinational logic (or an FSM) from the opcode and state.",
    "Microprogrammed control: each instruction maps to a sequence of microinstructions stored in a control memory (ROM) that set the control signals.",
    "Speed: hardwired is faster (no control-memory fetch); microprogrammed adds latency per step.",
    "Flexibility: microcode is easier to design for large, complex instruction sets and can be patched or updated after fabrication to fix bugs.",
    "Complexity: hardwired logic becomes hard to design and verify as the instruction set grows.",
    "RISC uses hardwired control because its small, regular instruction set makes the logic simple and it needs single-cycle, pipelined decode.",
]
P["56057988-c35c-4837-bf95-3d0780cd8f2d"] = [  # branch prediction
    "Deep pipelines resolve branches late, so each wrong guess flushes many stages.",
    "2-bit saturating counter per branch: states strongly/weakly taken and not taken; predict taken in the top two states; each outcome moves the counter one step.",
    "On a loop, a 1-bit predictor mispredicts twice per loop execution (exit and re-entry); the 2-bit counter needs two wrong outcomes to flip, so it mispredicts only once (at exit).",
    "Penalty: CPI = 1 + f_branch × miss rate × penalty, where penalty grows with pipeline depth.",
    "Example: 20% branches, 10% misses, 15-cycle penalty gives CPI = 1 + 0.2 × 0.1 × 15 = 1.3, a 30% slowdown.",
    "Hence deep pipelines need better predictors (correlating/tournament, branch target buffers) to keep their clock-speed advantage.",
]
