P["1fedad9a-7273-4186-8c0c-95d1f8d8869d"] = [  # struct by value vs pointer
    "By value: the whole struct is copied onto the stack each call — costly for large structs, but the callee cannot alter the caller's copy.",
    "By pointer: only an address is passed (fast); use const T * to prevent modification.",
    "Aliasing: with pointers, two parameters may refer to the same object (e.g. mat_mul(&c, &a, &c)), so writing the output can corrupt an input mid-computation; restrict or a temporary avoids this.",
    "Returning by value is reasonable for small types (e.g. struct Vec3 { double x, y, z; }): the copy is cheap, compilers often elide it, and code reads naturally: Vec3 s = add(a, b);",
    "For a large Matrix (e.g. 100 × 100 doubles = 80 kB) pass const pointers in and a pointer to the result out.",
    "Rule of thumb: by value for small, immutable-style data; by pointer for large data or when the function must modify it.",
]
P["208d803b-586c-474d-b976-ac5a961b815a"] = [  # priority queue choice
    "Unsorted array: insert O(1), extract-min O(n) (scan then remove).",
    "Sorted array: insert O(n) (shift elements), extract-min O(1) if kept in descending order (remove from the end).",
    "Sorted linked list: insert O(n) (walk to the position), extract-min O(1) at the head.",
    "Binary heap: insert O(log n) and extract-min O(log n), stored compactly in an array.",
    "For 10⁶ mixed operations the linear structures cost on the order of 10¹¹ steps in the worst case; the heap costs about 10⁶ × 20 = 2 × 10⁷.",
    "Recommend the binary heap (array-based min-heap): best balanced cost, cache-friendly, simple to implement.",
]
